1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,340
Some people believe the sky is blue,

2
00:00:02,340 --> 00:00:05,000
others believe that it's orange or purple.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,960
Right, and I don't know, it's just one example,

4
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:11,880
but who's to say it really is blue?

5
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,000
I mean, I remember it being blue,

6
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,360
but then maybe my memories are wrong.

7
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,840
Maybe we're in the matrix.

8
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,300
All of these concerns occur to people.

9
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, what can we do about this problem?

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,640
Well, there's a leading theoretical effort

11
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:30,200
to go and link up these two,

12
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,100
to go and say that there's going to be a connection

13
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,720
between objective and subjective probabilities

14
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:39,800
is called rational expectations, or RE for short.

15
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:49,600
So, how many people here have heard of RE before?

16
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:51,720
Right, so most of you, all right.

17
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:54,520
So, rational expectations,

18
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:56,200
actually the earliest papers by John Booth

19
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:57,920
in the early 1960s, although he didn't call it

20
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,280
rational expectations, but the idea's there.

21
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:02,440
And then it was actually in macroeconomics

22
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:05,920
that rational expectations first became a big deal.

23
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:07,360
This was during a period when people

24
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:09,040
were trying to understand inflation.

25
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:11,600
The early proceeding models were all based

26
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:13,320
upon adaptive expectations,

27
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:16,200
saying that people would just very mechanically

28
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:19,360
go and average recent inflation to form an opinion.

29
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:20,560
And rational expectation says,

30
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:22,120
no, that doesn't make sense.

31
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:25,340
People are going to take all the information into account.

32
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:28,160
And so, for example, if inflation has been rising,

33
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:31,080
they will perhaps forecast that it will continue to rise.

34
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:32,680
And then you shouldn't just go and take an average

35
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:35,040
of earlier periods, you should rather extrapolate

36
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:37,580
in the future, possibly, things like that.

37
00:01:37,580 --> 00:01:39,960
All right, so, a simple characterization

38
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:41,720
of rational expectations is to say

39
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:42,920
that you have rational expectations

40
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:46,420
as long as your judgments are, first of all, unbiased.

41
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:50,980
Which means that your mean error is zero.

42
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:52,680
Your mean error is zero.

43
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:55,040
Right, so, what would that be like?

44
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,820
Well, for example, when you're forecasting inflation,

45
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:59,360
sometimes you're too high, sometimes you're too low.

46
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:00,840
And what rational expectation says

47
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:03,160
is that your average error will be zero.

48
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:06,160
So your high value, the times when you overestimate inflation

49
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:08,400
will balance out the times that you underestimate inflation,

50
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:09,880
and when you average it out,

51
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:12,880
your forecast error will be zero.

52
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:14,920
Right, and furthermore, rational expectations

53
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:17,960
adds on another condition, which says

54
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:20,200
that mistakes are uncorrelated with available information.

55
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:25,200
So errors, uncorrelated with available information.

56
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:31,840
Right, now, this word available is a reasonable word

57
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:35,000
because, well, what counts as available?

58
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:36,960
In practice, when people started doing this in 70s,

59
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:38,280
available meant, well, was it in

60
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:40,160
the Wall Street Journal yesterday?

61
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:41,640
Then it's available.

62
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:43,680
On the other hand, if it's known

63
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:46,800
to only a few people working at Microsoft,

64
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:47,840
I don't know, was Microsoft?

65
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:50,240
I don't know, late 70s, was already Microsoft.

66
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:53,440
So it's only available to people

67
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:55,720
like on the board of directors of Microsoft,

68
00:02:55,720 --> 00:02:57,520
then that's not available.

69
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:00,560
But, all right, so anyway, this is the simple version,

70
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,360
and this is the one that is used for empirical testing.

71
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:08,620
But there is a deeper version of rational expectations,

72
00:03:08,620 --> 00:03:11,200
which you can see in theoretical papers

73
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:13,120
that apply rational expectations.

74
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:15,000
So the deeper version says

75
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:16,960
that you have rational expectations.

76
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:19,460
If your subject of probability distribution

77
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:21,820
matches the object of probability distribution.

78
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:27,220
And again, in a theoretical paper, you can make this true.

79
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:30,120
So theoretical paper, if you know what you're doing,

80
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:32,080
and a number of people do,

81
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:34,760
you can work it out so that you come up

82
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:36,560
with an internally consistent system

83
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:39,920
where there is, in fact, a distribution of outcomes,

84
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:41,560
and people's beliefs about those outcomes

85
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:43,880
have exactly the same distribution as the actual one,

86
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:46,620
and everything fits together like a glove.

87
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:49,160
Okay, so just like with Nash equilibrium,

88
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:51,480
you may remember when, so this is when,

89
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:55,200
if I assume that everyone else stays the same,

90
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:56,840
I wanna keep doing what I'm doing,

91
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:59,000
and this is true for every single person.

92
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,060
Similarly, with this deep version of rational expectations,

93
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:06,360
the idea is that given the beliefs that everybody has,

94
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:08,400
there is an objective distribution,

95
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:10,360
and when there is that objective distribution,

96
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:12,760
everybody's beliefs about it match.

97
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:16,000
So it's all the pieces fit together perfectly.

98
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,760
All right, now, of course, you cannot test that empirically,

99
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:21,600
but what you can do is test for these things,

100
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:24,080
like unbiasedness, or see whether the errors

101
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:27,300
are uncorrelated with available information.

102
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:31,160
All right, now, rational expectations,

103
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:35,120
real, inspires a very standard modeling technique,

104
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:36,820
and any time you've ever talked to an economist

105
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:37,660
where you've said, well, look,

106
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:39,200
the problem is people have really bad information

107
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:41,840
about this, and you've gotten frustrated

108
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:44,560
because, at least I've experienced it many times,

109
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:46,320
well, people don't know that, right?

110
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:50,560
Because, well, if you take rational expectations for granted

111
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:51,880
then you say, all right, well, you're saying

112
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:53,700
that there's really bad information, right?

113
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:56,680
Well, this is the truth here, capital T truth here,

114
00:04:56,680 --> 00:05:00,200
all right, and if people have really good information,

115
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:02,680
then their belief distribution will be

116
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:05,360
very tightly clustered around the truth,

117
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:07,240
but if people have bad information,

118
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:09,400
then their belief distribution will be

119
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,440
widely distributed around the truth.

120
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:16,440
So, this is bad info, this is good info,

121
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:21,340
and of course, this is perfect info.

122
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:28,480
All right, so, in the rational expectations model,

123
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:32,060
there is endless room for degrees of knowledge,

124
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:36,460
but it still rules out a lot of complaints about the world.

125
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:38,440
Like, if you were to just say, well, look,

126
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:41,760
you know, the problem with private auto safety

127
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:43,560
is most people don't realize that they,

128
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:45,760
that an accident could ever happen to them.

129
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:49,000
All right, have you ever heard that from this?

130
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,320
See, Jonathan Gruber's Obamacare graphic novel,

131
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:53,720
he says, look, a lot of young people don't get insurance

132
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:56,440
because they don't know that they could get sick.

133
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:58,320
It was actually, like, that's almost a quote,

134
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:00,160
and I'm like, Jonathan Gruber wrote this?

135
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:02,920
So, you think that young people literally believe

136
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:05,120
they can't get sick, this is a common view?

137
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:08,400
Or maybe they actually have the rational view

138
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:10,120
that they're unlikely to get sick.

139
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:13,400
Another possibility why someone might not buy insurance,

140
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:15,280
but it sounds a lot better to just say,

141
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:18,160
look, these poor people don't have the slightest clue

142
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:20,560
that they are mortal.

143
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:21,640
All right, you might have heard your parents

144
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:23,480
say this in high school, the problem with these kids

145
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:26,080
is they don't realize they could die.

146
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:29,740
Well, it's not literally true, I'm good.

147
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:33,720
Anyway, so, so this is the standard way

148
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:37,280
that economists, the economists model uncertainty,

149
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:39,120
which is to say, look, everyone's unbiased,

150
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:40,800
whether you got perfect information,

151
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:43,120
good information, bad information,

152
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:47,840
the average view in all cases is still literal truth,

153
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:51,440
but, but, the less you know,

154
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:53,080
the greater the variance will be.

155
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:55,960
So, with bad information, you got this big variance,

156
00:06:55,960 --> 00:06:57,880
good information, you got a small variance,

157
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:01,400
perfect information, you got a zero variance, and so on.

158
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:05,880
Right, now, there are many myths about rationalizations,

159
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,920
and sometimes I feel like some of my colleagues

160
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:11,840
spread the myths, so, I'll, possibly they've stopped,

161
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:14,800
I don't know, but, any case, it is a myth to say

162
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:17,040
that rational expectations assumes perfect knowledge

163
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:18,800
or assumes away ignorance.

164
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:21,720
It doesn't, rational expectations totally allows

165
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:25,400
for ignorance, totally allows for perfect information,

166
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:29,080
but it does require that it look a certain way.

167
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:32,520
So it is a theory about what extreme ignorance looks like.

168
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:37,520
So, if you had an incredibly wide distribution of views,

169
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:43,280
you, that would still be, you would be highly ignorant,

170
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:45,480
you would think almost anything could happen,

171
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:47,120
but as long as your average view were true,

172
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:49,480
then you could still have rational expectations.

173
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:53,480
Okay, so it doesn't rule out error,

174
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:56,640
in fact, any time you see a deviation from truth,

175
00:07:56,640 --> 00:07:57,760
that is an error.

176
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:00,560
Right now, sometimes Austrians will say,

177
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:02,480
well, that's not a real error.

178
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:04,280
A real error isn't when you think something

179
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:05,800
and it's not true.

180
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:08,920
A real error is something other than that,

181
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:10,880
and it's like something worse,

182
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:13,560
what's worse than thinking something when it's not true?

183
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:16,400
It's like thinking something that's super not true,

184
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:18,880
being like a million miles from the truth, no, no, no,

185
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:21,360
that's just a regular but severe error.

186
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:23,800
There's a higher kind of error, right?

187
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:26,760
And I have written about this, but ultimately,

188
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:28,240
I cannot plausibly explain it

189
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:30,240
because it doesn't make any sense to me.

190
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:33,560
All right, so again, the best thing people will do

191
00:08:33,560 --> 00:08:36,240
is someone will say, look, this talks about

192
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:39,520
the known unknowns, but there's also the unknown unknowns.

193
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:40,360
You may have heard about that,

194
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:43,320
but we know something about these allegedly

195
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:45,200
unknown unknowns.

196
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:48,360
For example, it's like, well, so what are the odds

197
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:50,680
of an unknown unknown mattering?

198
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:52,760
Again, you have an opinion about that.

199
00:08:52,760 --> 00:08:56,120
How often in your life have unknown unknowns caused a problem?

200
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:57,480
In that sense, you could still say,

201
00:08:57,480 --> 00:09:00,080
well, they're really known unknown unknowns.

202
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:02,200
So while I didn't think about them in advance,

203
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:04,960
I had a general idea about things going wrong.

204
00:09:04,960 --> 00:09:09,080
So let's see, so like on,

205
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:11,880
number of times on bets, the bets that I made,

206
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:15,240
there's been an issue, like in the real world,

207
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:17,080
which has allowed me to win, and then someone will say,

208
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:18,960
well, did you anticipate that that issue

209
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:19,800
would allow you to win?

210
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:21,520
I was like, no, I have no clue about that issue.

211
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:24,040
But I did know there would be issues.

212
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:25,720
I knew there would be issues.

213
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:28,520
Like when I have a bet about whether any country

214
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:32,480
in the EU will officially withdraw by January 1st, 2020.

215
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:34,360
I chose the language officially withdraw

216
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:36,760
because I knew that there might be lots

217
00:09:36,760 --> 00:09:39,480
of different unofficial attempts to withdraw,

218
00:09:39,480 --> 00:09:42,400
or preliminary measures which don't go anywhere,

219
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:45,440
or several steps, and then they stop and delay.

220
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:47,600
Right, and I said officially withdraw by that date

221
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:50,080
because I knew there's tons of stuff going on there.

222
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:52,800
Many things which I would have no ability to articulate,

223
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:55,200
but I still know they're out there.

224
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:56,960
Right, or if you have ever, say,

225
00:09:56,960 --> 00:09:59,360
to fill out some bureaucratic paperwork,

226
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:01,040
and then someone says, well, what are the odds

227
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:02,600
you think this is gonna work out for you,

228
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:04,480
that you get your visa or whatever,

229
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:06,120
and you have a probability on that,

230
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:09,640
even though you have very little specific idea

231
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:10,480
about what the problems could be.

232
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:11,640
It's just like, well, it'll be just like

233
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:13,780
some bureaucratic problem or other.

234
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:18,040
As long as you can think of it like that,

235
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:19,680
then it would be counterintuitive.

236
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:20,960
Noted that counterintuitive would come

237
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:22,600
under rationalized negations.

238
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:25,200
All right, now, rationalized negations

239
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:27,820
also does not assume that information is free.

240
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:29,440
In fact, there's an underlying assumption

241
00:10:29,440 --> 00:10:30,980
that it's not free because if it were free,

242
00:10:30,980 --> 00:10:33,720
why wouldn't everyone have perfect information?

243
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:35,420
If you could get a large and infinite amount for free,

244
00:10:35,420 --> 00:10:37,200
then everyone should just be there.

245
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:39,720
So presumably the reason why there is this Y,

246
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:41,280
why there's a Y distribution,

247
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:44,120
is because it's not free to acquire information.

248
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:45,280
Right, and of course, doesn't assume

249
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:47,480
that cognition is free either.

250
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:50,340
So you can think about, for example,

251
00:10:50,340 --> 00:10:52,880
the cost of solving the game of chess,

252
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:55,560
a cost no one in the universe, as far as we know,

253
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:57,760
has ever paid because it's still unknown

254
00:10:57,760 --> 00:10:59,800
what the solution to chess is.

255
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:01,920
Last time I checked, checkers was solved.

256
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:03,200
I don't know what the solution was,

257
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:06,000
but checkers was solved to mathematicians'

258
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:07,000
eternal satisfaction.

259
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:09,240
Can I ask a question?

260
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:10,120
Oh, sure, sure.

261
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:12,480
So it seems like the way this is modeled,

262
00:11:12,480 --> 00:11:14,200
it's like there's a number,

263
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:15,680
and you can be above or below that number.

264
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:16,520
Yes.

265
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:18,920
What about maybe where something's located?

266
00:11:18,920 --> 00:11:23,840
Like if someone's searching for gold, or, you know,

267
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:26,200
it doesn't seem like there's a variance

268
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:27,840
of belief about that.

269
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:29,080
Hmm, well, so if it's location,

270
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:31,360
then you just have longitude and latitude,

271
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:34,200
and you could have rational locations about both,

272
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:36,680
and then on average, you're at exactly the right point,

273
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:38,640
but then there's a spread around that.

274
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:40,800
I mean, yeah.

275
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:44,280
But why would we expect the mean to be the true value?

276
00:11:44,280 --> 00:11:45,960
Ah, so.

277
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:46,800
You see what I'm saying?

278
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:48,160
Like there's no reason.

279
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:51,560
Well, I mean, so that is a totally fair question,

280
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:54,120
and so John Muth, on the original article,

281
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:55,720
at a footnote, giving three arguments

282
00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:57,240
for why we should believe it,

283
00:11:57,240 --> 00:11:58,920
the arguments are not awful,

284
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:00,760
but they're also not very convincing.

285
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:06,920
They're sort of like, yeah, kind of, maybe, arguments.

286
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:08,480
Now, and then for a long time,

287
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:09,640
hardly anyone bothered with it,

288
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:11,520
and then psychology and economics came along

289
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:12,840
and started presenting a lot of evidence

290
00:12:12,840 --> 00:12:15,520
that actually a lot of errors don't look like that.

291
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:17,480
So, like, as to why should we really believe it,

292
00:12:17,480 --> 00:12:19,120
that's a totally fair question.

293
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:20,880
Now, one thing is, like, often you could go

294
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:24,480
and transform the question so that the thing

295
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:28,880
that you are thinking about for errors

296
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:31,360
is just on a different axis or something like that,

297
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:34,000
so maybe it wouldn't literally be location,

298
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:35,680
maybe it would be rational expectations

299
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:37,080
about the amount of time it would take

300
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:39,760
for you to discover gold, for example,

301
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:42,160
and then you transform it from location to time,

302
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:44,040
and of course, what you really wanna think about,

303
00:12:44,040 --> 00:12:46,320
well, what's the economically relevant variable?

304
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:47,720
I mean, economically isn't really, like,

305
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:49,880
who cares what the actual geography is?

306
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:51,840
It's really, like, maybe time, or better yet,

307
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:54,280
how about cost, expected value of the cost

308
00:12:54,280 --> 00:12:56,440
of finding the gold, which is what people

309
00:12:56,440 --> 00:12:57,880
would actually care about.

310
00:12:57,880 --> 00:12:59,520
See how that might work.

311
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:01,640
All right, so, I mean, plenty of examples here,

312
00:13:01,640 --> 00:13:03,240
so, like, attending graduate school,

313
00:13:03,240 --> 00:13:05,680
no one knows for sure how you'll do,

314
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:07,520
but rational expectation says, on average,

315
00:13:07,520 --> 00:13:09,280
you'll correctly estimate how well you do in the program

316
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:11,240
and what completion will do it for you.

317
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:13,040
Or it could be running a movie, right?

318
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:15,040
So, until you see it, there's no way to really know

319
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:17,440
for sure how much you're going to like it,

320
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:19,880
but rational expectation says that, on average,

321
00:13:19,880 --> 00:13:21,920
your prospective ratings will equal

322
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:23,800
your retrospective ratings.

323
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:26,480
So, now you think about something like Netflix reviews,

324
00:13:26,480 --> 00:13:29,320
where you go and you've got a rating

325
00:13:29,320 --> 00:13:30,440
you think you're gonna give it,

326
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:32,120
there's a rating you actually give it,

327
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:34,720
and then the question is, on average, are you correct?

328
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:37,320
And if you are, then you've got rational expectations.

329
00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:40,360
All right, and then, of course, the same would go

330
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:44,440
for correlation of any errors with available information.

331
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:47,800
So, if you know the genre, the actors, the director,

332
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:49,080
then there shouldn't be any correlation

333
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:52,040
between your errors and that available information.

334
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:53,480
All right, so, if it's prominently displayed,

335
00:13:53,480 --> 00:13:55,880
at least that would seem to be a problem.

336
00:13:56,920 --> 00:13:59,440
All right, Donald Whitman has an example

337
00:13:59,440 --> 00:14:01,480
on pork barrel spending.

338
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:03,520
So, back in the 70s and 80s,

339
00:14:03,520 --> 00:14:04,960
there were many public choice economists who said,

340
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:07,840
well, look, do you know why government wastes so much money?

341
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:10,240
Because nobody knows what it's doing.

342
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:12,280
So, of course, there's tons of spending

343
00:14:12,280 --> 00:14:14,520
wasted on stupid bridges to nowhere

344
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:16,920
when voters don't know what's going on.

345
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:19,600
All right, that sounds like a plausible story,

346
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:23,280
but Whitman pointed out it's not a rational expectation story

347
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:25,960
because rational expectation says, look,

348
00:14:25,960 --> 00:14:27,640
sometimes people overestimate

349
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:28,720
how much government is spending,

350
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:29,960
sometimes they'll underestimate it,

351
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:31,760
but on average, they will be correct.

352
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:33,160
And as long as you know, on average,

353
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:35,040
how much money government is wasting,

354
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:37,840
then you should have the proper level of interest

355
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:39,800
in a politician who says,

356
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:42,280
I'm gonna go and try to save you some money.

357
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:45,640
So, if in fact government's saving very little money,

358
00:14:45,640 --> 00:14:47,240
then some politician comes along to that

359
00:14:47,240 --> 00:14:48,800
and says, eh, we'll just figure problems

360
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:50,000
and just save you some money.

361
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,320
On the other hand, if on average,

362
00:14:51,320 --> 00:14:53,280
the correct view is that government's wasting a lot,

363
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:56,040
then you'll be interested in that person, and so on.

364
00:14:57,360 --> 00:14:59,240
All right, now let's give an example

365
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:01,240
of applied rationalization,

366
00:15:01,240 --> 00:15:03,640
so testing for the rational expectations

367
00:15:03,640 --> 00:15:05,640
of beliefs about the economy.

368
00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:08,760
So, as I said, the first area where rational expectations

369
00:15:08,760 --> 00:15:11,240
made a big splash was in macro.

370
00:15:11,240 --> 00:15:14,400
There was no intellectual reason for this at all.

371
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:17,280
All right, so even though rational expectations

372
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:20,120
is psychologically tied to macro,

373
00:15:20,120 --> 00:15:22,080
there was never any reason for that,

374
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:25,680
and now, so anytime there's a theory paper

375
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:27,560
that isn't specifically about rational expectations

376
00:15:27,560 --> 00:15:29,720
not holding, normally we'll go

377
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:31,760
and build rational expectations in the model,

378
00:15:31,760 --> 00:15:34,560
whether it's macro or macro, it doesn't matter.

379
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:36,200
But anyway, so in the 70s,

380
00:15:36,200 --> 00:15:37,640
there were a bunch of empirical tests

381
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:39,400
made on things like inflation forecasts

382
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:41,600
to test rational expectations.

383
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:43,320
So how do you do this?

384
00:15:43,320 --> 00:15:44,400
Well, very simple.

385
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:47,240
So suppose you've got a bunch of forecasts of inflation.

386
00:15:47,240 --> 00:15:49,680
Of course, a forecast means that it's what somebody says

387
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:52,080
about inflation at what time?

388
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:55,080
Before the inflation's observed,

389
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:57,280
otherwise it's not a forecast, all right?

390
00:15:57,280 --> 00:16:00,280
So call that F, and then we're gonna estimate this

391
00:16:00,280 --> 00:16:04,160
as a constant plus a coefficient times I,

392
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:06,800
where I is actual observed inflation later on.

393
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:08,840
So we can have a bunch of forecasts about inflation,

394
00:16:08,840 --> 00:16:11,680
and even today, there would be plenty of newsletters

395
00:16:11,680 --> 00:16:13,480
and things like that with different investment advisors

396
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:15,240
give you their forecast of inflation.

397
00:16:15,240 --> 00:16:18,360
In the 70s, these might be in little mail,

398
00:16:18,360 --> 00:16:19,760
I think they'd actually get in the mail,

399
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:21,120
piece of paper that you might spend

400
00:16:21,120 --> 00:16:22,400
hundreds of dollars a year for

401
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:24,920
if you think the person actually knows something.

402
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:26,880
All right, so you've had so many ways.

403
00:16:26,880 --> 00:16:30,960
So I is the actual observed inflation after the forecast.

404
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:34,080
Of course, you have to wait until after the forecasts

405
00:16:34,080 --> 00:16:38,040
have been, the time, so you have to wait

406
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:40,200
for the time period of the forecast to occur

407
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:42,560
before you can go and find out what really happened,

408
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:44,000
and then compare.

409
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:45,440
All right, now, rational expectations

410
00:16:45,440 --> 00:16:48,040
makes a specific prediction.

411
00:16:48,040 --> 00:16:50,680
It says that alpha will be zero and beta will be one.

412
00:16:52,120 --> 00:16:54,200
All right, and you can get a lot of data,

413
00:16:54,200 --> 00:16:56,320
and you can then go and test that hypothesis

414
00:16:56,320 --> 00:16:57,560
and see how it turns out.

415
00:16:58,600 --> 00:17:00,880
All right, so that's one example.

416
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:03,760
And so in the 70s, usually it didn't work exactly,

417
00:17:03,760 --> 00:17:05,400
but especially given that they didn't have

418
00:17:05,400 --> 00:17:06,960
a lot of that many data points,

419
00:17:06,960 --> 00:17:08,920
a lot of students didn't seem to be too bad.

420
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:11,880
All right, so that's just one example.

421
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:12,920
And of course, this could be anything.

422
00:17:12,920 --> 00:17:15,240
That could be interest rates, it could be GDP,

423
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:17,120
anything that people are forecasting.

424
00:17:17,120 --> 00:17:19,880
You would go and collect a bunch of forecasts,

425
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:21,280
and then collect a bunch of facts,

426
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:22,920
and then test the forecast against the facts

427
00:17:22,920 --> 00:17:24,120
and see what's going on.

428
00:17:25,280 --> 00:17:27,200
Right, make sense?

429
00:17:27,200 --> 00:17:29,880
Okay, all right, now, another case

430
00:17:29,880 --> 00:17:31,800
that's very interesting to me

431
00:17:31,800 --> 00:17:36,360
is the rationalizations for beliefs about economics itself.

432
00:17:36,360 --> 00:17:38,440
So it seems to me that one of the main goals

433
00:17:38,440 --> 00:17:41,720
of intro econ classes is to correct students'

434
00:17:41,720 --> 00:17:44,200
misconceptions about economics.

435
00:17:44,200 --> 00:17:46,920
Now, what is a misconception?

436
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:48,240
Well, to me, a misconception is something

437
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:50,680
that doesn't fit in that model, right?

438
00:17:50,680 --> 00:17:52,560
You know, so like a misconception about free trade

439
00:17:52,560 --> 00:17:54,960
isn't maybe free trade is better than it really is.

440
00:17:54,960 --> 00:17:56,400
Some people think free trade is better than it is.

441
00:17:56,400 --> 00:17:58,080
Other things, it's worse than it really is.

442
00:17:58,080 --> 00:18:00,880
Misconception about free trade to me

443
00:18:00,880 --> 00:18:03,640
is something like people think that imports are bad

444
00:18:03,640 --> 00:18:06,520
and exports are good, and the ideal world is one

445
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:07,560
where you don't import anything

446
00:18:07,560 --> 00:18:09,320
and you export all your stuff.

447
00:18:09,320 --> 00:18:11,640
That to me is a misconception, right?

448
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:13,200
Or it could be a misconception to think

449
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:15,720
that trade must be based upon absolute advantage,

450
00:18:15,720 --> 00:18:18,960
when in fact, trade is based upon, what again?

451
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:20,720
Comparative advantage, right?

452
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:25,720
So these things, so, well, to me, are misconceptions,

453
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:29,120
or, you know, misconception if someone thinks

454
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:31,360
that price controls have no side effects.

455
00:18:32,440 --> 00:18:35,160
Right, and you're like, does anyone really think that?

456
00:18:35,160 --> 00:18:38,000
Well, I know one person who thought that until he was 17,

457
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,560
which is me, because I had no idea

458
00:18:40,560 --> 00:18:43,080
there could be any negative side effect of any price control.

459
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:45,600
Never heard it, never occurred to me independently.

460
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:47,720
Furthermore, I never, I have no memory

461
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:49,240
of any human being ever saying

462
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:51,480
there could be anything, any negative effect.

463
00:18:52,560 --> 00:18:55,920
Possibly I was just ignoring everyone.

464
00:18:55,920 --> 00:18:57,560
Maybe every time I walked down the hall,

465
00:18:57,560 --> 00:18:59,720
people were saying, my God, minimum wage

466
00:18:59,720 --> 00:19:01,720
and its negative side effects.

467
00:19:01,720 --> 00:19:03,240
And I was just lost in my own world,

468
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:04,880
not listening to these things being said,

469
00:19:04,880 --> 00:19:08,280
but it just appeared that these things were never mentioned,

470
00:19:08,280 --> 00:19:10,800
and instead that everyone around me

471
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:14,240
had the ultra-naive view that the higher the minimum wage,

472
00:19:14,240 --> 00:19:16,800
the better for workers, and the only reason

473
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:18,000
anyone could oppose the minimum wage

474
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
is because you don't care about workers.

475
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:24,880
And now to me, that would count as a misconception, right?

476
00:19:24,880 --> 00:19:28,440
A view which is not just some people

477
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:30,000
overestimate the negative side effects,

478
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:33,040
some underestimate them, but rather a systematic tendency

479
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:37,380
to think that the negative side effects just don't exist.

480
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:40,320
All right, so anyway, it seems to me

481
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:42,480
that most intro econ classes try to correct

482
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:45,220
students' preexisting economic misconceptions.

483
00:19:46,320 --> 00:19:49,000
Now, of course, it could be that it's the economists

484
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,440
who are wrong and students know what they're talking about,

485
00:19:51,440 --> 00:19:52,640
at least on average.

486
00:19:52,640 --> 00:19:55,200
Might be, well, maybe yes.

487
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:57,580
So there's some people who are naive protectionists,

488
00:19:57,580 --> 00:19:59,040
but there's other people who think

489
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:23,040
the free trade will go.


